     These functions provide a simple interface for asynchronous execution of
     code.

     The function ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee() is used to create new queues.  The argu‐
     ments to ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee() include a name that should be unique, a set
     of malloc(9) flags that specify whether the call to mmaalllloocc() is allowed
     to sleep, a function that is called from ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee() when a task
     is added to the queue, and a pointer to the memory location where the
     identity of the thread that services the queue is recorded.  The function
     called from ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee() must arrange for the queue to be pro‐
     cessed (for instance by scheduling a software interrupt or waking a ker‐
     nel thread).  The memory location where the thread identity is recorded
     is used to signal the service thread(s) to terminate--when this value is
     set to zero and the thread is signaled it will terminate.  If the queue
     is intended for use in fast interrupt handlers ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee__ffaasstt()
     should be used in place of ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee().

     The function ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ffrreeee() should be used to free the memory used by
     the queue.  Any tasks that are on the queue will be executed at this time
     after which the thread servicing the queue will be signaled that it
     should exit.

     Once a taskqueue has been created, its threads should be started using
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ssttaarrtt__tthhrreeaaddss(), ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ssttaarrtt__tthhrreeaaddss__ccppuusseett() or
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ssttaarrtt__tthhrreeaaddss__iinn__pprroocc().  ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ssttaarrtt__tthhrreeaaddss__ccppuusseett()
     takes a _c_p_u_s_e_t argument which will cause the threads which are started
     for the taskqueue to be restricted to run on the given CPUs.
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ssttaarrtt__tthhrreeaaddss__iinn__pprroocc() takes a _p_r_o_c argument which will cause
     the threads which are started for the taskqueue to be assigned to the
     given kernel process.  Callbacks may optionally be registered using
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__sseett__ccaallllbbaacckk().  Currently, callbacks may be registered for the
     following purposes:

     TASKQUEUE_CALLBACK_TYPE_INIT      This callback is called by every thread
                                       in the taskqueue, before it executes
                                       any tasks.  This callback must be set
                                       before the taskqueue's threads are
                                       started.

     TASKQUEUE_CALLBACK_TYPE_SHUTDOWN  This callback is called by every thread
                                       in the taskqueue, after it executes its
                                       last task.  This callback will always
                                       be called before the taskqueue struc‐
                                       ture is reclaimed.

     To add a task to the list of tasks queued on a taskqueue, call
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee() with pointers to the queue and task.  If the task's
     _t_a___p_e_n_d_i_n_g field is non-zero, then it is simply incremented to reflect
     the number of times the task was enqueued, up to a cap of USHRT_MAX.
     Otherwise, the task is added to the list before the first task which has
     a lower _t_a___p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y value or at the end of the list if no tasks have a
     lower priority.  Enqueueing a task does not perform any memory allocation
     which makes it suitable for calling from an interrupt handler.  This
     function will return EPIPE if the queue is being freed.

     When a task is executed, first it is removed from the queue, the value of
     _t_a___p_e_n_d_i_n_g is recorded and then the field is zeroed.  The function
     _t_a___f_u_n_c from the task structure is called with the value of the field
     _t_a___c_o_n_t_e_x_t as its first argument and the value of _t_a___p_e_n_d_i_n_g as its sec‐
     ond argument.  After the function _t_a___f_u_n_c returns, wakeup(9) is called on
     the task pointer passed to ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee().

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee__ffllaaggss() accepts an extra _f_l_a_g_s parameter which
     specifies a set of optional flags to alter the behavior of
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee().  It contains one or more of the following flags:

     TASKQUEUE_FAIL_IF_PENDING    ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee__ffllaaggss() fails if the task
                                  is already scheduled for execution.  EEXIST
                                  is returned and the _t_a___p_e_n_d_i_n_g counter value
                                  remains unchanged.

     TASKQUEUE_FAIL_IF_CANCELING  ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee__ffllaaggss() fails if the task
                                  is in the canceling state and ECANCELED is
                                  returned.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee__ttiimmeeoouutt() function is used to schedule the enqueue
     after the specified number of _t_i_c_k_s.  The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee__ttiimmeeoouutt__ssbbtt()
     function provides finer control over the scheduling based on _s_b_t, _p_r, and
     _f_l_a_g_s, as detailed in callout(9).  If the _t_i_c_k_s argument is negative, the
     already scheduled enqueueing is not re-scheduled.  Otherwise, the task is
     scheduled for enqueueing in the future, after the absolute value of _t_i_c_k_s
     is passed.  This function returns -1 if the task is being drained.  Oth‐
     erwise, the number of pending calls is returned.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccaanncceell() function is used to cancel a task.  The _t_a___p_e_n_d_i_n_g
     count is cleared, and the old value returned in the reference parameter
     _p_e_n_d_p, if it is non-NULL.  If the task is currently running, EBUSY is re‐
     turned, otherwise 0.  To implement a blocking ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccaanncceell() that
     waits for a running task to finish, it could look like:

           while (taskqueue_cancel(tq, task, NULL) != 0)
                   taskqueue_drain(tq, task);

     Note that, as with ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn(), the caller is responsible for en‐
     suring that the task is not re-enqueued after being canceled.

     Similarly, the ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccaanncceell__ttiimmeeoouutt() function is used to cancel the
     scheduled task execution.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn() function is used to wait for the task to finish,
     and the ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn__ttiimmeeoouutt() function is used to wait for the sched‐
     uled task to finish.  There is no guarantee that the task will not be en‐
     queued after call to ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn().  If the caller wants to put the
     task into a known state, then before calling ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn() the caller
     should use out-of-band means to ensure that the task would not be en‐
     queued.  For example, if the task is enqueued by an interrupt filter,
     then the interrupt could be disabled.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn__aallll() function is used to wait for all pending and
     running tasks that are enqueued on the taskqueue to finish.  Tasks posted
     to the taskqueue after ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn__aallll() begins processing, including
     pending enqueues scheduled by a previous call to
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee__ttiimmeeoouutt(), do not extend the wait time of
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn__aallll() and may complete after ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn__aallll() re‐
     turns.  The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__qquuiieessccee() function is used to wait for the queue to
     become empty and for all running tasks to finish.  To avoid blocking in‐
     definitely, the caller must ensure by some mechanism that tasks will
     eventually stop being posted to the queue.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__bblloocckk() function blocks the taskqueue.  It prevents any en‐
     queued but not running tasks from being executed.  Future calls to
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee() will enqueue tasks, but the tasks will not be run un‐
     til ttaasskkqquueeuuee__uunnbblloocckk() is called.  Please note that ttaasskkqquueeuuee__bblloocckk()
     does not wait for any currently running tasks to finish.  Thus, the
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__bblloocckk() does not provide a guarantee that ttaasskkqquueeuuee__rruunn() is
     not running after ttaasskkqquueeuuee__bblloocckk() returns, but it does provide a guar‐
     antee that ttaasskkqquueeuuee__rruunn() will not be called again until
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__uunnbblloocckk() is called.  If the caller requires a guarantee that
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__rruunn() is not running, then this must be arranged by the caller.
     Note that if ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn() is called on a task that is enqueued on a
     taskqueue that is blocked by ttaasskkqquueeuuee__bblloocckk(), then ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn()
     can not return until the taskqueue is unblocked.  This can result in a
     deadlock if the thread blocked in ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn() is the thread that is
     supposed to call ttaasskkqquueeuuee__uunnbblloocckk().  Thus, use of ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn() af‐
     ter ttaasskkqquueeuuee__bblloocckk() is discouraged, because the state of the task can
     not be known in advance.  The same caveat applies to
     ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ddrraaiinn__aallll().

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__uunnbblloocckk() function unblocks the previously blocked
     taskqueue.  All enqueued tasks can be run after this call.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__mmeemmbbeerr() function returns 1 if the given thread _t_d is part
     of the given taskqueue _q_u_e_u_e and 0 otherwise.

     The ttaasskkqquueeuuee__rruunn() function will run all pending tasks in the specified
     _q_u_e_u_e.  Normally this function is only used internally.

     A convenience macro, TTAASSKK__IINNIITT(_t_a_s_k, _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y, _f_u_n_c, _c_o_n_t_e_x_t) is provided
     to initialise a _t_a_s_k structure.  The TTAASSKK__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() macro generates
     an initializer for a task structure.  A macro TTIIMMEEOOUUTT__TTAASSKK__IINNIITT(_q_u_e_u_e,
     _t_i_m_e_o_u_t___t_a_s_k, _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y, _f_u_n_c, _c_o_n_t_e_x_t) initializes the _t_i_m_e_o_u_t___t_a_s_k
     structure.  The values of _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y, _f_u_n_c, and _c_o_n_t_e_x_t are simply copied
     into the task structure fields and the _t_a___p_e_n_d_i_n_g field is cleared.

     Five macros TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEECCLLAARREE(_n_a_m_e), TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEEFFIINNEE(_n_a_m_e, _e_n_q_u_e_u_e,
     _c_o_n_t_e_x_t, _i_n_i_t), TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__FFAASSTT__DDEEFFIINNEE(_n_a_m_e, _e_n_q_u_e_u_e, _c_o_n_t_e_x_t, _i_n_i_t), and
     TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEEFFIINNEE__TTHHRREEAADD(_n_a_m_e) TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__FFAASSTT__DDEEFFIINNEE__TTHHRREEAADD(_n_a_m_e) are used
     to declare a reference to a global queue, to define the implementation of
     the queue, and declare a queue that uses its own thread.  The
     TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEEFFIINNEE() macro arranges to call ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee() with the
     values of its _n_a_m_e, _e_n_q_u_e_u_e and _c_o_n_t_e_x_t arguments during system initiali‐
     sation.  After calling ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee(), the _i_n_i_t argument to the macro
     is executed as a C statement, allowing any further initialisation to be
     performed (such as registering an interrupt handler, etc.).

     The TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEEFFIINNEE__TTHHRREEAADD() macro defines a new taskqueue with its own
     kernel thread to serve tasks.  The variable _s_t_r_u_c_t _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e
     _*_t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___n_a_m_e is used to enqueue tasks onto the queue.

     TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__FFAASSTT__DDEEFFIINNEE() and TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__FFAASSTT__DDEEFFIINNEE__TTHHRREEAADD() act just like
     TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEEFFIINNEE() and TTAASSKKQQUUEEUUEE__DDEEFFIINNEE__TTHHRREEAADD() respectively but
     taskqueue is created with ttaasskkqquueeuuee__ccrreeaattee__ffaasstt().

   PPrreeddeeffiinneedd TTaasskk QQuueeuueess
     The system provides four global taskqueues, _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___f_a_s_t,
     _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___s_w_i, _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___s_w_i___g_i_a_n_t, and _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___t_h_r_e_a_d.  The
     _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___f_a_s_t queue is for swi handlers dispatched from fast interrupt
     handlers, where sleep mutexes cannot be used.  The swi taskqueues are run
     via a software interrupt mechanism.  The _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___s_w_i queue runs without
     the protection of the _G_i_a_n_t kernel lock, and the _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___s_w_i___g_i_a_n_t
     queue runs with the protection of the _G_i_a_n_t kernel lock.  The thread
     taskqueue _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___t_h_r_e_a_d runs in a kernel thread context, and tasks run
     from this thread do not run under the _G_i_a_n_t kernel lock.  If the caller
     wants to run under _G_i_a_n_t, he should explicitly acquire and release _G_i_a_n_t
     in his taskqueue handler routine.

     To use these queues, call ttaasskkqquueeuuee__eennqquueeuuee() with the value of the
     global taskqueue variable for the queue you wish to use.

     The software interrupt queues can be used, for instance, for implementing
     interrupt handlers which must perform a significant amount of processing
     in the handler.  The hardware interrupt handler would perform minimal
     processing of the interrupt and then enqueue a task to finish the work.
     This reduces to a minimum the amount of time spent with interrupts dis‐
     abled.

     The thread queue can be used, for instance, by interrupt level routines
     that need to call kernel functions that do things that can only be done
     from a thread context.  (e.g., call malloc with the M_WAITOK flag.)

     Note that tasks queued on shared taskqueues such as _t_a_s_k_q_u_e_u_e___s_w_i may be
     delayed an indeterminate amount of time before execution.  If queueing
     delays cannot be tolerated then a private taskqueue should be created
     with a dedicated processing thread.

